-- title:
--   History $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal H_t$
-- statement:
--   For random losses $\ell_1,\ell_2,\dots$ on a measurable space $\Omega$, the **history** at round $t$ is the $\sigma$-algebra
--
--   $$\mathcal H_t=\sigma(\ell_1,\dots,\ell_{t-1}),$$
--
--   generated by the losses of rounds $1,\dots,t-1$. $\mathcal H_1$ is the trivial $\sigma$-algebra.
-- source:
--   Dani, Hayes, Kakade, Stochastic Linear Optimization under Bandit Feedback, COLT 2008, §5.2, p. 8

import Mathlib
open Matrix

namespace StochLinOpt.ConfBall2

/-- **The history σ-algebra `H_t`** (Dani, Hayes, Kakade, *Stochastic Linear Optimization under Bandit Feedback*, COLT 2008, §5.2, p. 8: "Ht denotes the complete history of
the game on rounds 1, . . . , t − 1, that is, the σ-algebra generated by ℓ1, . . . , ℓt−1").

`lossHistory ℓ t = σ(ℓ_1, …, ℓ_{t−1})`, the supremum of the σ-algebras pulled back by the losses
of rounds `1 ≤ τ < t`.

**Formalization Note** Rounds are 1-based; `lossHistory ℓ 1` is the trivial σ-algebra. -/
def lossHistory {Ω : Type*} (ℓ : ℕ → Ω → ℝ) (t : ℕ) : MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  ⨆ τ ∈ Finset.Ico 1 t, MeasurableSpace.comap (ℓ τ) inferInstance

end StochLinOpt.ConfBall2


